## Executive thesis

`gpu-rack-qualifier` is best positioned as an AI infrastructure qualification control plane, not a report generator.

It answers a narrow and commercially important question:

> Which GPU nodes are safe candidates for multi-node training, which should be limited to inference or single-node work, and which should be reviewed before scheduling because qualification evidence shows communication, topology, firmware, GPU-count, or sensor risk?

The product's value comes from the gap between "the rack was delivered" and "the rack is safe to expose to expensive AI workloads through the scheduler." A GPU rack can be powered on and still waste high-value capacity if any of the following are wrong:

- NCCL all-reduce correctness
- pairwise NCCL bandwidth
- GPU-to-GPU or GPU-to-NIC topology
- expected GPU count
- driver/CUDA/VBIOS consistency
- ECC or MIG mode
- BMC health
- fan health
- temperature and power sensor state
- evidence coverage and parser quality

The repo turns this evidence into review-only Slurm-facing labels, quarantine recommendations, portfolio summaries, drift reports, handoff bundles, and business-impact counters. That is a core workflow for AI cluster commissioning and ongoing rack qualification.

## External market and technical context

The web research supports the repo's problem framing.

- NVIDIA's GB200 NVL72 page describes a rack-scale, liquid-cooled system with 36 Grace CPUs and 72 Blackwell GPUs in a single NVLink domain. It also describes 130 TB/s of low-latency GPU communication and explicitly says NVLink and liquid cooling help overcome communication bottlenecks. Source: https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/gb200-nvl72/
- NVIDIA's NCCL documentation says NCCL provides topology-aware inter-GPU communication primitives, including AllReduce, and supports fast collectives over multiple GPUs within and across nodes. It also notes that efficient neural network training is possible with NCCL's multi-GPU and multi-node communication. Source: https://docs.nvidia.com/deeplearning/nccl/user-guide/docs/overview.html
- Slurm documentation supports the repo's scheduler-facing strategy: node extra data and constraints can be used to filter jobs based on node attributes, while `scontrol` can view or modify Slurm configuration and state. Source: https://slurm.schedmd.com/extra_constraints.html and https://slurm.schedmd.com/scontrol.html
- DMTF Redfish is a standard for simple, secure, human-readable and machine-capable infrastructure management; DMTF also publishes Redfish guidance for liquid cooling, power distribution, firmware update, telemetry, and schemas. This makes Redfish-like BMC sensor snapshots a credible evidence input. Source: https://www.dmtf.org/standards/redfish
- IEA's 2025 Energy and AI report says there is "no AI without energy," that AI-focused data centers are power hungry, and that a typical AI-focused data center can consume as much electricity as 100,000 households. It also says global data center investment nearly doubled since 2022 and reached about half a trillion dollars in 2024, while data center electricity consumption is projected to more than double by 2030. Source: https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-and-ai/executive-summary
- Uptime Institute's 2024 outage analysis says 54% of respondents' most recent significant, serious, or severe outage cost more than $100,000, 16% cost more than $1 million, power issues are the most common cause of serious/severe data center outages, network issues are the largest single cause of IT service outages, and four in five serious outages could have been prevented with better management, processes, and configuration. Source: https://intelligence.uptimeinstitute.com/resource/annual-outage-analysis-2024

The realistic ROI claim is not "this tool guarantees training success." It is:

> The tool reduces wasted accelerator hours, bad scheduler placement, failed commissioning cycles, unplanned drains, thermal/fan risk, firmware drift, and support escalation time by making qualification evidence deterministic, reviewable, scheduler-facing, and auditable.

## What the local repo actually contains

The README describes `gpu-rack-qualifier` as a local-first CLI that converts GPU communication, topology, and sensor evidence into scheduler-facing node labels and quarantine recommendations.

The tool consumes:

- NCCL single-node all-reduce output
- user-supplied pairwise NCCL CSV
- `nvidia-smi topo -m`
- optional `nvidia-smi topo -p2p n`
- `nvidia-smi -q -x` XML
- Redfish-like BMC sensor JSON
- optional node inventory YAML
- optional qualification policy YAML

It writes:

- Slurm node labels CSV
- quarantine/review CSV
- commented Slurm feature snippet
- commented drain-review script
- summary JSON
- evidence bundle JSON
- markdown explanation
- rack portfolio
- baseline and drift reports
- business impact report
- coverage report
- handoff bundle ZIP

It explicitly does not schedule jobs, manage Slurm, update firmware, reboot hosts, run cluster-wide tests, connect to BMCs, store credentials, call external services, or enforce quarantine. That review-only boundary is commercially important because it makes the output usable in real operations without handing control of production infrastructure to a new tool.

