## Personas

### AI infrastructure director

Owns the business outcome: getting GPU capacity into productive service without creating avoidable training failures, outage risk, or executive escalation.

### Cluster operations lead

Owns rack commissioning and day-to-day operational readiness. Needs deterministic evidence before broad scheduling.

### Scheduler administrator

Owns Slurm partitions, labels, constraints, drain workflows, and safe placement policy.

### ML platform lead

Owns user-facing training and inference platforms. Needs to prevent research and production teams from landing jobs on weak or risky nodes.

### Fabric and network engineer

Owns GPU communication paths, NIC paths, switch/fabric groups, and pairwise performance.

### Facilities and power/cooling engineer

Owns thermal, power, airflow, fan, liquid-cooling, and physical environment risk.

### Firmware and systems engineer

Owns driver, CUDA, VBIOS, ECC, MIG, and hardware identity consistency.

### Procurement and finance partner

Owns utilization, value-at-risk, vendor acceptance, and capital-efficiency narratives.

### Vendor/OEM support manager

Needs concise evidence bundles and reproduction context when escalating node or rack issues.

### Security/compliance or audit lead

Needs local-only, reviewable, redacted, hash-backed evidence without credentials or external calls.

### Data science / model training owner

Cares about job reliability, throughput, multi-node scaling, and avoiding failed long-running jobs.

## Top business stories

### 1. Commission a new GPU rack without exposing users to bad multi-node training nodes

Personas:

- AI infrastructure director
- cluster operations lead
- scheduler administrator
- ML platform lead

Story:

A newly delivered 64-node GPU rack is physically installed and visible to the scheduler. The business pressure is to release capacity immediately because model teams are waiting. The operations team runs `gpu-rack-qualifier` before opening the rack broadly.

Generated evidence:

- only 3 of 64 nodes received `PASS`
- 24 nodes received multi-node training labels
- 40 nodes lacked a multi-node label
- 23 nodes were quarantine-review candidates
- 8 nodes were avoid-multinode candidates
- 5 nodes were inference-only candidates

Business impact:

Instead of treating "rack installed" as "rack ready," the team opens only the nodes with sufficient evidence for the right workload class. Multi-node training lands only on nodes with compatible evidence; single-node and inference work can still use some lower-risk capacity. The business avoids failed commissioning cycles, wasted training time, and user distrust in the platform.

Why the ROI is credible:

The tool does not claim all non-PASS nodes are broken. It creates safe placement lanes. That prevents a common expensive failure mode: using a partially qualified rack for all workload types.

### 2. Prevent weak pairwise communication from silently degrading distributed training

Personas:

- fabric and network engineer
- ML platform lead
- data science / model training owner
- scheduler administrator

Story:

Distributed training jobs are sensitive to slow peers. A few weak fabric paths can pull down the effective throughput of the whole job. The generated run found 5 weak pairwise nodes, 6 weak topology nodes, 6 P2P missing nodes, and 8 avoid-multinode nodes.

Business impact:

The scheduler admin can label those nodes `gpu_avoid_multinode` instead of letting them join large training jobs. The fabric engineer receives specific questions: "Does this node have weaker pairwise NCCL performance with all peers or only specific peers?" and "Is this topology expected for the server model, or does it indicate a cabling/riser/fabric issue?"

ROI path:

Avoiding one poorly placed multi-node job can save hours or days of expensive accelerator time. More importantly, it protects user trust: model teams see that the platform distinguishes "usable GPU" from "safe for distributed training."

### 3. Stop correctness-error nodes before they corrupt or invalidate expensive training runs

Personas:

- cluster operations lead
- ML platform lead
- data science / model training owner
- vendor/OEM support manager

Story:

The generated output found 6 NCCL correctness-error blockers. The tool labeled those nodes `QUARANTINE` and produced `gpu_do_not_schedule` recommendations.

Business impact:

Correctness failures are qualitatively worse than ordinary slowness. A slow node wastes time; a correctness-risk node can invalidate a run and send teams into painful debugging. The tool gives operations a defensible reason to keep those nodes out of scheduling until GPU, driver, and fabric health are validated.

ROI path:

The avoided cost is not just accelerator hours. It includes model-team debugging time, bad experiment conclusions, delayed releases, and vendor escalation churn.

